We did not manage to extract a prediction in the correct format. Gold: ['The required condition is \\boxed{MR=SRMC}, or marginal revenue is equal to short-run marginal cost.'], Pred: ["Okay, so I have this question about a firm's profit maximization condition in the short-term. Let me try to remember what I know about this. \n\nI remember that when a firm is in the short run, they have fixed capital. That means they can't change their level of physical capital in the short term because they can't hire more workers or buy more machinery quickly. So, the number of machines and factories stays the same.\n\nNow, the question is about the algebraic condition for maximizing profits in this situation. Profit maximization is a key concept in economics, and I think the main condition is about the relationship between marginal revenue and marginal cost.\n\nIn the short run, the firm can only adjust its output level by changing the quantity produced. It can’t vary capital because of fixed costs. So, the only variable input is labor, which affects the marginal cost.\n\nI recall that profit is calculated as total revenue minus total cost. Total revenue is the price per unit times the quantity sold, and total cost includes both fixed and variable costs. But since in the short run fixed costs don't vary with output, the marginal cost (the additional cost of producing one more unit) equals the variable cost per unit. \n\nTo maximize profit, the firm should produce at the point where the additional revenue from selling one more unit (marginal revenue, MR) equals the marginal cost of producing that unit. That makes sense because if marginal revenue is higher than marginal cost, the firm can still make a profit on each additional unit. But if marginal revenue is lower than marginal cost, the firm is giving up more money on revenue by producing that extra unit. So, the firm should stop producing when marginal revenue equals marginal cost.\n\nPutting this together, the condition is that marginal revenue equals marginal cost (MR = MC). This is the fundamental condition for profit maximization under short-run equilibrium when there's variable costs, because fixed costs are constant in the short run and can be ignored in the optimization process.\n\nI think that's the algebraic condition. So, the profit-maximizing condition is when marginal revenue equals marginal cost, or MR = MC.\n</think>\n\nThe profit-maximizing condition for a firm in the short run is when marginal revenue equals marginal cost, as it indicates the point where additional revenue covers the additional cost of producing an extra unit, leading to maximum profit.\n\n**Answer:**\nMR = MC"]
