We did not manage to extract a prediction in the correct format. Gold: ['The required condition is \\boxed{MR=SRMC}, or marginal revenue is equal to short-run marginal cost.'], Pred: ["Alright, so I have to figure out what algebraic condition describes a firm that's producing output to maximize profits in the short term. Okay, I remember from my economics class that in the short run, a firm's output decisions are based on the relationship between marginal cost (MC) and marginal revenue (MR). \n\nWait, profit maximization. Right, so the basic idea is that a firm should produce up to the point where marginal cost equals marginal revenue because at that point, the additional revenue from producing another unit equals the additional cost, which is the peak of profit. But since firms can't always control both MC and MR, there must be conditions that determine whether this maximum is achievable.\n\nOh, right! If the firm is in the short run, which means it can't vary all its inputs, like in the long run where it can all change, then it can't continue increasing production indefinitely. So, the firm is limited in its capital stock. That means it's constrained by its fixed capital. So, the MC per unit of capital and MR per unit of capital must balance each other.\n\nWait, what's the term for that? I think it's Shark's condition. Remember, the Sharks' condition is used when a firm has fixed capital. It states that the firm should choose the level of output where the marginal cost per unit of capital equals the marginal revenue per unit of capital. So, MC_K = MR_K.\n\nLet me break that down. MC_K is the marginal cost per unit of capital, which is the derivative of the total cost with respect to capital (dC/dK). Similarly, MR_K is the derivative of total revenue with respect to capital (dTR/dK). So, when dC/dK equals dTR/dK, the firm is at the profit-maximizing output level for given fixed capital.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the condition is MC_K = MR_K, which is the Shark's condition, also known as the Shephard condition. Therefore, the algebraic condition is MC_K equals MR_K.\n\nAnother way to think about it is that when the cost of getting one more unit of output (measured by capital) doesn't exceed the revenue it brings (also measured by capital), the firm has no incentive to expand either further or stop producing because it's not making useful contributions. That makes sense because if MC was higher than MR, producing more would increase profit, and if MC was lower, it would reduce profit. So, the equality point is the sweet spot where the firm can't do better by expanding further or cutting back.\n\nI should make sure I didn't mix any terms here. Sometimes, some might call it the Shephard condition, but all point to the same idea. So, yeah, MC equals MR per unit of capital is the answer.\n</think>\n\nThe algebraic condition that describes a firm's profit-maximizing output level in the short term, given fixed capital, is when the marginal cost per unit of capital equals the marginal revenue per unit of capital. \n\n**Answer:** MC_K = MR_K"]
